Context: transverse small vibrations of an elastic rod or lamina (Euler–Bernoulli beam). Euler has separated variables and written the 'regular' (single-mode, synchronous) vibration as y(s,t) = C sin(ζ + tω) S(s), where ω = √(2g/k). Here k is the length of the isochronous simple pendulum, and S(s) = α e^{s/f} + β e^{−s/f} + γ sin(s/f) + δ cos(s/f), with f⁴ = b c² k. S is the general solution of the spatial ODE S'''' = S/f⁴.

This page lists the s-derivatives needed for the end conditions (Corollary 2):
- y_s = (C/f) sin(·)(α e^{s/f} − β e^{−s/f} + γ cos(s/f) − δ sin(s/f));
- y_ss = (C/f²) sin(·)(α e^{s/f} + β e^{−s/f} − γ sin(s/f) − δ cos(s/f));
- y_sss = (C/f³) sin(·)(α e^{s/f} − β e^{−s/f} − γ cos(s/f) + δ sin(s/f));
- y_ssss = (C/f⁴) sin(·) S = y/f⁴.

Corollary 3 gives the time derivatives. The velocity is y_t = Cω cos(ζ + ωt) S. The acceleration is y_tt = −(2g/k) C sin(ζ + ωt) S = −(2g/k) y. Combining with y_ssss = y/(b c² k) gives y_tt = −2g b c² y_ssss, which is the beam equation in Euler's units (2g convention). So Euler can say all prescribed conditions are satisfied.

Scholion 1 begins the remark that the many arbitrary constants allow infinite variety. On the next page the boundary conditions at the ends (free, pinned, clamped) fix the ratios α:β:γ:δ. They also yield a transcendental frequency equation in a/f, hence k and the pitch.
